# One rule decides line endings for everything Git considers text.
#
# eol=lf matters as much as text=auto. On its own, text=auto normalizes to LF inside the repository
# but checks out with the platform default, which is CRLF on Windows. That is not cosmetic here: it
# is what editorconfig-checker reports on .vscode/settings.json, which was committed with CRLF and
# failed the gate on every run until the file was renormalized.
#
# Because this covers every path, per-type "text eol=lf" lines are redundant and are not listed.
# Only genuine exceptions appear below.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The one place CRLF is correct: the Windows batch interpreter requires it.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Files Git must not treat as text. "binary" is shorthand for "-text -diff": no line-ending
# conversion, and no attempt to show a textual diff.
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.ico    binary
*.webp   binary
*.pdf    binary

*.mp3    binary
*.mp4    binary
*.mov    binary
*.wav    binary
*.webm   binary

*.zip    binary
*.tar    binary
*.tar.gz binary
*.tgz    binary
*.gz     binary
*.7z     binary
*.rar    binary

# The local D1 database under .wrangler/state is a SQLite file. It is gitignored, and this only
# says so in a second place rather than by accident.
*.sqlite binary
*.db     binary

# Diff drivers change how a change is displayed, not how it is stored, so these are unaffected by
# the rule above and still carry their weight: Git uses them to label hunks with the enclosing
# function or section.
*.ts      diff=ts
*.json    diff=json
